A||||6||rodmcneill||Steering rack bolt strength?||||1||
Z||000000||rodmcneill||12-14-2001||11:46 PM||||I read that you are supposed to replace the steering rack bolts when you replace it. The dealer does not have them and I seem to be having problems locating bolts of the right strength. The originals are metric with a 9.8 on the head, I have found stainless steel bolts at the hardware store with U V A2 80 on the head are these strong enough? This must be a new code as I have not seen it before. Should I just wire brush the old bolts and reuse?||||reg||1
Z||000001||wcapman||12-14-2001||11:58 PM||||You will NEVER find stainless steel with the same strength as Grade 7 or 9 carbon steel.  Stainless should be avoided because of chloride stress cracking.  Road salts can make the bolts literally break off.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||12-15-2001||12:09 AM||||Idealy they should be replaced. I'm sure people reuse decent looking ones.<P>In metric that's called a class number. 8.something is about Grade 5 in SAE. 10.9 is about SAE Grade 8. You've got something in between. If you replace, use the next stronger if you can't get exact. That means 10.9 or Grade 8.<P>I'm not sure who in the U.S. carries allot of metric bolts. Some car parts stores carry loose bolts. <A HREF="http://www.imperialinc.com" TARGET=_blank>www.imperialinc.com</A>  carries allot of Grade 8 stuff but I'm not sure if any SAE bolt is a suitable fit in the hole. Many SAE bolts are very close in fit to metric in a non threaded hole so it is posible you could use them if you can find ones that fill the hole.<P>You might want to check the MotorMite master catalog at a local parts store. They carry all sorts of stuff. They might have suitable bolts.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mark||12-15-2001||06:52 PM||||Pep Boys carries a pretty good selection of metric hardware.  I'm pretty sure that includes class 10.9.  I would think that NAPA and the other big chains may also have metric hardware "behind the counter".||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierospeeder||12-16-2001||03:05 AM||||I had two of the bolts broke in half when i had to drop my rack.<P>I chiseled the nuts off and used some of those c clip/bolt set.  I cant remember what the bolt rating was.<P>In the future, i might weld a nut back on, but i have to figure out how to get a welder to do that in such a hard location.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Neal||12-16-2001||12:37 PM||||I reused my bolts, they came off easy enough and all looked good after a minute with a wire brush.<P>Are there any bolt supply shops in your area?  There is a few around me that have saved my butt a couple of times.||||reg||14
Z||000006||MrPAW||12-17-2001||09:37 AM||||9.8 is the metric equivalent of Grade 5.<BR>10.9 is the same as Grade 8.<P>As wcapman said stainless bolts are weaker than both grade 5 and 8 and should be avoided.<P>Most of the time the manufacturer will recommend replacing the bolt if they use locking nuts. The crimp style locking nut used on most suspension bolts tend to tear off the tops of the threads when removed making it more likely for the nut to strip off when re-installed.<P>I usually re-use bolts if they are not corroded and the threads still look fully formed.<P>If you do decide to replace the bolts, and the originals had locking nuts, try to get locking nuts with the new ones so they will not vibrate loose.  Loose bolts fatigue and break much more easily.<P>Paul||||reg||1
